<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fructuosus of Tarragona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 259</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Saint Fructuosus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fructuosus was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Tarraconensis, and a Christian martyr. During the persecution under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> he was burned alive with his deacons Augurius and Eulogius in the city's amphitheatre in January 259. The account of his martyrdom is one of the earliest testimonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and a church was later built in the amphitheatre in his memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fructuosus-of-tarragona.jpg" alt="Fructuosus of Tarragona" width="247" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fructuosus.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
